<h2 id="flow-heading">Primary, Secondary &amp; Tertiary Packaging Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Primary Packaging Conveyors</h3>
<p>Primary packaging conveyors handle individual products during filling, sealing, capping, and labeling. They need precise positioning, gentle handling, and consistent spacing. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyors</a> stop products at exact points for fill heads, cappers, labelers, and vision inspection. <a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">Tabletop chain conveyors</a> carry bottles, cans, and jars because the flat chain surface resists tipping.</p>
<p>Product-contact conveyors in food plants need <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food-grade construction</a> with food-contact belt materials and stainless frames. Pharmaceutical lines need GMP-compliant designs.</p>
<h3>Secondary Packaging Conveyors</h3>
<p>Secondary packaging conveyors move sealed cases, cartons, bundles, trays, and shrink-wrapped multipacks, so food-contact rules typically do not apply, though some food safety audit schemes require washdown capability in these zones too. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a> (gravity, powered, and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a>) carry most of this traffic because flat, rigid case bottoms ride well on rollers. See <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing and case conveyors</a> for this stage in detail.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Laning and collating conveyors</a> split single-file product into lanes for case packing. <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">Reject conveyors</a> divert out-of-spec product, checkweigher failures, and metal detector rejects.</p>
<h3>Tertiary Packaging Conveyors</h3>
<p>Tertiary conveyors move palletized loads from palletizer discharge through stretch wrapping to staging and shipping. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyors</a> carry the heaviest loads on the line, and <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">chain-driven live roller (CDLR)</a> is the common type. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> (turntables, right-angle and chain transfers) route pallets between lanes, storage rows, and dock doors.</p>
<h2 id="layout-heading">Packaging Line Layout and Integration Points</h2>
<p>Layout sets floor space use, operator and maintenance access, and fork truck routes. Conveyor crossovers, guarding, and emergency stops must meet <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, the U.S. safety standard for conveyors. Three layout patterns cover most lines.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Straight line:</strong> filler to palletizer in one run. Fewest transfers and simplest conveyor design, but needs the most floor length. Suits single-product lines with few changeovers.</li>
<li><strong>U-shape:</strong> the line starts and ends on the same wall, so receiving and shipping share a side. Shortens fork truck travel and lets operators see both ends. Needs 180-degree turns using <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curves</a>.</li>
<li><strong>L-shape:</strong> one 90-degree turn fits the line to the building. Common when primary packaging runs along one wall and secondary and tertiary along the next.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Key Integration Points</h3>
<p>Most conveyor problems show up where conveyors hand product to a machine. Each integration point needs the right conveyor type, speed matching, and controls coordination.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Integration Point</th>
<th>Conveyor Type</th>
<th>Key Requirement</th>
<th>Common Problem</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Filler to capper</td>
<td>Belt / tabletop chain</td>
<td>Speed match, gentle handling</td>
<td>Tipping, spacing errors</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Labeler to case packer</td>
<td>Belt + accumulation</td>
<td>Accumulation buffer</td>
<td>Case packer starved after upstream stops</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Case packer to sealer</td>
<td>Powered roller / MDR</td>
<td>Tracking, orientation</td>
<td>Skewed cases, open flaps</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sealer to palletizer</td>
<td>Roller + laning</td>
<td>Pattern formation</td>
<td>Gaps and wrong orientation at pattern forming</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Palletizer to stretch wrap</td>
<td><a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor</a> / <a href="/conveyor-types/pallet-conveyors/">CDLR</a></td>
<td>Heavy load, low speed</td>
<td>Pallet queueing, load shift</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="accumulation-heading">Accumulation and Buffering for Packaging Lines</h2>
<p>Every machine on a packaging line stops periodically for jam clears, changeovers, roll changes, and minor faults. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> absorb those stops so upstream and downstream machines keep running. Without them, a short jam at the case sealer stops the filler, the labeler, and the palletizer.</p>
<h3>Sizing Buffers</h3>
<p class="formula"><strong>Accumulation Length (ft) = Buffer Time (min) x Products/min x Product Pitch (ft)</strong></p>
<p>Product pitch is product length plus the gap between products. Set buffer time from the line's own stop data: the buffer should ride through the frequent short stops of the machine it protects without stopping its neighbors. Long stops such as changeovers and breakdowns are usually handled by stopping the line, not by buffer.</p>
<p>Buffers only refill if machines around them can run faster than the line rate. Most lines use a V-curve: the critical machine, usually the filler, has the lowest rated speed, and machines upstream and downstream are rated progressively faster so they can refill and drain buffers after a stop.</p>
<h3>Buffering Options for High-Speed Lines</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Buffer Type</th>
<th>Product Order</th>
<th>Space Use</th>
<th>Best For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Inline accumulation (ZPA, minimum pressure)</td>
<td>FIFO</td>
<td>Horizontal length</td>
<td>Cases, printed or fragile cartons</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mass or bi-directional accumulation table</td>
<td>FIFO or LIFO, by design</td>
<td>Wide floor area</td>
<td>Bottles, cans, jars in mass</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">Dynamic spiral buffer</a></td>
<td>FIFO (inline) or LIFO (offline)</td>
<td>Vertical, small footprint</td>
<td>Large buffers where floor space is tight</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="/conveyor-types/serpentine-conveyors/">Serpentine / storage conveyor</a></td>
<td>FIFO</td>
<td>Compact floor area</td>
<td>Long buffer length in a rectangular footprint</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Choose by product stability, whether product order must be kept (for lot or date code control), and available floor or vertical space. Zero-pressure (ZPA) keeps products from touching and costs the most. Minimum-pressure allows light contact and suits rigid cases. Back-pressure accumulation suits only heavy, rigid products that tolerate contact. See <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer conveyors</a> for FIFO and LIFO buffer design in depth.</p>
<h2 id="controls-heading">Speed Matching and Line Control</h2>
<p>Every conveyor must match speed with the machines on either side. Mismatches cause jams, spacing errors, and machine starvation. The line PLC, or the individual machine controllers, coordinates conveyor speeds, accumulation zones, and start and stop sequences. <a href="https://www.omac.org/packml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OMAC PackML</a> defines a standard machine state model that simplifies coordination when machines come from different suppliers.</p>
<h3>Variable Frequency Drives (VFDs)</h3>
<p><a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">VFDs</a> set motor speed by varying frequency and voltage, so speed changes from the PLC or HMI without gearbox changes. They also soft-start conveyors, which reduces mechanical shock on belts and chains.</p>
<h3>Photo-Eye Sensors</h3>
<p>Photo-eyes detect product at accumulation zones, transfer points, and machine infeeds. They trigger conveyor start and stop, zone release in MDR accumulation, and jam faults. Food-grade lines need stainless or sealed housings rated to the appropriate IP level.</p>
<h3>Line Speed Calculation</h3>
<p class="formula"><strong>Speed (FPM) = Products/min x (Product Length + Gap in inches) / 12</strong></p>
<p>Add speed headroom above peak throughput so the conveyor never limits the machines. In <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>, the controller adjusts speed from sensor inputs and machine status.</p>
<h2 id="cost-heading">Packaging Line Conveyor Cost Drivers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total footage:</strong> more length costs more, but not linearly. Longer runs spread fixed costs (drives, controls) over more footage.</li>
<li><strong>Transfers, merges, and diverts:</strong> each is a discrete unit and costs far more per foot than straight conveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Accumulation:</strong> zero-pressure MDR accumulation costs more per foot than simple powered roller transport.</li>
<li><strong>Material:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food-grade stainless</a> costs substantially more than painted carbon steel.</li>
<li><strong>Controls integration:</strong> PLC programming, HMI screens, networking, and VFDs are a major share of project cost, not an add-on.</li>
<li><strong>Installation:</strong> structural supports, electrical work, and commissioning add to equipment cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors by conveyor type.</p>
