<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What conveyors are used in a packaging line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt and <a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">tabletop chain conveyors</a> for primary packaging, <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller</a> and MDR conveyors for cases, <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> between machines, <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> for turns and lane changes, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyors</a> for finished loads. Most lines also use reject, laning, and indexing conveyors.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between primary, secondary, and tertiary packaging conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>Primary:</strong> handles individual products during filling, capping, and labeling. Requires gentle handling and precise positioning.</p>
<p><strong>Secondary:</strong> carries cases, cartons, and bundles through case packing, sealing, and shrink wrapping.</p>
<p><strong>Tertiary:</strong> moves palletized loads to stretch wrappers and shipping docks. Heaviest loads, lowest speeds.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How much accumulation do I need between packaging machines?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Size each buffer from your line's stop data: enough product to ride through the frequent short stops of the machine it protects without stopping its neighbors. Length = Buffer time (min) x Products per minute x Product pitch (ft). Long stops such as changeovers are usually handled by stopping the line.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What are the best conveyor buffering systems for high-speed packaging lines?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>No single type is best. Inline zero-pressure accumulation suits cases and fragile cartons. Mass and bi-directional tables hold large volumes of round containers. <a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">Dynamic spiral buffers</a> store product vertically and can keep FIFO order. Serpentine conveyors fit long buffer length into limited floor space. Choose by product stability, order requirements, available space, and the length of stops the buffer must absorb.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What conveyor speed do I need for my packaging line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Speed (FPM) = Products/min x (Product length + gap, in inches) / 12. Add headroom above peak throughput so the conveyor never limits the machines, and run conveyors feeding a machine at or slightly above that machine's rate.</p>

</div>
</div>
